        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Adrianople</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">9 August 378</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Within a few hours' march of Adrianople (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>), in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a>; the exact site is disputed, with three locations proposed north and east of the city</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Eastern Roman army under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>, perhaps as many as 15,000 men by some modern estimates, and Gothic rebels led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a>, joined by the cavalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alatheus">Alatheus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saphrax">Saphrax</a> and by Alans, put by several modern historians at 12,000 to 15,000</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Roman army was routed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> died, though how is uncertain; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> then failed to take Adrianople.</span></div>
        </div>
