    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> marched out against the advice of some of his officers and without waiting for the western emperor Gratian, whose reinforcements were on their way, and he rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a>'s offer of peace and an alliance the day before.</li><li>The Gothic cavalry came back from foraging after the fighting had begun, and surrounded Roman troops who were already in disarray; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a>'s talks had gained the time needed for its return.</li><li>Historians disagree about the battle's weight: Charles Oman saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> replacing Roman infantry, while T. S. Burns called it mainly a fight of infantry against infantry.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>'s body was never found, and two stories of his death circulated: that he died anonymously on the field, or that he was burned in a peasant's cottage after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> set it on fire.</p></aside>
    </section>

