    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">376</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> led by Alavivus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a> are admitted across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> as allies and revolt after hardships under the commanders Lupicinus and Maximus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">377</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> fight the Battle of the Willows, which ends as a bloody draw</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">378</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 30 May and sends Sebastianus to reorganise the armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">378</span><span class="rf-text">On 8 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a> offers peace and an alliance in exchange for Roman territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> refuses</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">378</span><span class="rf-text">On 9 August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> march out from Adrianople and are routed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and their cavalry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> dies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">378</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> try and fail to take Adrianople</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">379</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> is made emperor on 19 January</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">382</span><span class="rf-text">Peace is declared on 3 October and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> settle inside the Empire as foederati</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-adrianople" data-v="28fbf4981d" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies face each other below the hill</b> The Goths held a wagon circle on a hilltop, and the Romans, tired and thirsty after a march, came up facing it. Both sides drew up in battle order, and the Gothic cavalry was away.</li><li><b>The shield-archers attack without orders</b> Units of the imperial guard began the fight on their own and attacked the Goths without support. They were easily pushed back.</li><li><b>The left wing reaches the wagons</b> The shield-archers fell back while the Roman left wing pressed on until it reached the circle of wagons. By then, the accounts say, it was too late.</li><li><b>The Gothic cavalry returns and surrounds the Romans</b> The Gothic cavalry came back from foraging and arrived to support the infantry. It surrounded the Roman troops, who were already in disarray after the failed assault.</li><li><b>The Romans are driven back and routed</b> The Romans retreated to the base of the hill, where they could not manoeuvre in their heavy armour. Casualties and exhaustion led to a rout, and the cavalry kept up the attack until nightfall.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/valens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valens.jpg" alt="Valens" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:INC-3015-a_%D0%A1%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%B4._%D0%92%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82_II._%D0%9E%D0%BA._364_%D0%B3._(%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%81).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">АНО &quot;Международный нумизматический клуб, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 376 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, led by Alavivus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a>, asked to be allowed to settle in the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> had displaced them. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>, hoping they would become farmers and soldiers, admitted them as allies (foederati). Once across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the dishonesty of the Roman provincial commanders Lupicinus and Maximus led the newcomers to revolt after many hardships. Officials under Lupicinus sold off much of the food meant for the Goths, and Gothic families sold children into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> for dog meat. At Marcianople Lupicinus ordered Fritigern and Alavivus held hostage and their retainers killed, then released Fritigern, and the Goths rebelled.</p><p>Valens asked the western emperor Gratian for reinforcements, and Gratian sent the general Frigeridus and the leader of his guards, Richomeres. For the next two years there was a series of running battles with no clear victories for either side, among them the Battle of the Willows in 377, a bloody draw in which the Roman left wing began to collapse before it was reinforced. Valens had meanwhile been kept in the East by negotiations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and a Saracen uprising.</p><p>In 378 Valens decided to take control himself and assembled troops from his own resources in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and from the reserves of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 30 May and appointed Sebastianus, newly arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, to reorganise the armies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Gratian's army, meanwhile, was delayed when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lentienses">Lentienses</a>, part of the Alamanni, attacked across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>'s army may have drawn on three field armies: the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, which may have lost heavily in 376 and 377, and the 1st and 2nd Armies in the Emperor's Presence, which had been committed to the Persian frontier in 376 and sent west in 377 and 378. It included veterans and consisted of seven legions of 700 to 1,000 men each, with a cavalry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> and the imperial guard, the Scholae. Some modern historians put the Roman strength at as many as 15,000 men, 10,000 infantry and 5,000 cavalry. Another estimate puts the original force at 40,000. Warren Treadgold's figures for the three armies are for 395 and include units formed after Adrianople, and not all three armies are likely to have fought together.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> were probably in two armies south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a> led one, recruited largely from the Therving exiles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alatheus">Alatheus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saphrax">Saphrax</a> led another, recruited largely from the Greuthung exiles, whose cavalry took part with Alans. The Gothic armies were mostly infantry, with some cavalry. Roman scouts counted 10,000 Goths, a figure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> dismissed as too low, since Alatheus and Saphrax's men were away. Several modern historians put the Gothic strength at 12,000 to 15,000, and more recent scholarship mostly holds that the two armies were similar in size.</p><p>Valens fortified his camp at Adrianople with ditches and ramparts. He learned on 6 August that about 10,000 Goths were advancing from a position 25 kilometres to the north. Part of the Gothic cavalry was out foraging at a distance, and Valens's estimate of the numbers left it out.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 8 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a> sent an emissary proposing peace and an alliance in exchange for Roman territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>, sure of victory because of his supposed numerical superiority, rejected it. Richomeres had brought a letter from Gratian asking Valens to wait for reinforcements, and Valens's officers also advised waiting, but Valens decided to fight. On the morning of 9 August he left the imperial treasury and administration under guard in Adrianople and marched north, arriving around noon after eight miles over difficult terrain.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> came tired and dehydrated to the Gothic camp on a hilltop. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, except for their cavalry, held a circle of wagons that held their families and possessions. Fritigern wished to delay the Romans until the cavalry came back. The Goths burned the fields so that smoke harassed the Romans, and negotiations for an exchange of hostages began, which exasperated the Roman soldiers but gained Fritigern time.</p><p>Some Roman units began the battle without orders, believing victory would be easy. The imperial scholae of shield-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> under the Iberian prince Bacurius attacked and, lacking support, were pushed back. The Roman left wing then reached the wagon circle, but too late: the Gothic cavalry returned from foraging and surrounded the Romans, who were already in disarray. They fell back to the foot of the hill, unable to manoeuvre in their heavy armour and with long shields, and casualties, exhaustion and pressure turned into a rout. The killing went on until nightfall.</p><p>The emperor was abandoned by his guards; most of the cavalry fled.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to Ammianus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> marched at once to Adrianople and tried to take it, and failed. Many Roman soldiers who had not been let into the city fought the besiegers below the walls. A third of the Roman army retreated, and the losses were beyond counting. By another account two thirds of the army lay dead, and the total loss may have been as high as 26,000 of an original 40,000. Many officers fell, among them Sebastianus, Traianus, the tribune Aequitius and thirty-five senior officers, while Ricomer, Victor and Saturninus escaped.</p><p>The Goths, together with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and Alans, went on to Perinthus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where they were fended off, and then plundered the countryside again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> had died without a successor in the East. The magister militum Julius had the Goths near the frontier lured together and killed, and when word of this reached the Goths in the interior provinces in 379, some rioted, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> put down the riots and killed Goths there too.</p><p>Theodosius was made emperor on 19 January 379, and the sources say little of how. Peace was declared on 3 October 382. The Goths recognised the emperor's authority and served as foederati, but unlike earlier practice they were given land in Scythia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> and possibly Macedonia, under their own authority and not dispersed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alatheus">Alatheus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saphrax">Saphrax</a> are not mentioned again in the ancient histories.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum.jpg" alt="Notitia Dignitatum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodl_Canon.Misc.378_roll159B_frame28.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The main source is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>, whose detailed contemporary account of the lead-up to the battle, from the Roman side, forms the end of his history. He names the officers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a>'s army and a few of its units. A list of the army's units survives in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a>, which dates from after Theodosius, so an order of battle cannot be drawn up: many units it names were formed after Adrianople or moved there later.</p><p>Where the sources differ, Ammianus has Sebastianus urging an immediate attack and Victor advising a wait for Gratian, while Eunapius has Sebastianus saying they should wait. The sources are few for the years 379 to 382. Of Valens's end nothing certain is known: the story that he was burned in a cottage circulated after the battle.</p><p>Historians dispute the site, which lay within a few hours' march of the city. One thesis puts it north of the city between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tundzha">Tonzos</a> and Hebros rivers, another east of the Tonzos near Muratçalı, and a third east of the city near Demirhanlı. The numbers on both sides are estimates. Older works held that overwhelming Gothic numbers, Gothic cavalry and the stirrup explain the Gothic victory; more recent works mostly hold that the armies were of similar size, that the infantry mattered more than the cavalry, and that neither side used stirrups until the 6th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodosius-i.jpg" alt="Theodosius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Theodosius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">www.livius.org, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-376-382">Gothic War</a> of 376 to 382, the battle is often considered the start of the events which led to the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> in the 5th century, although the war's significance is widely debated. It was the worst Roman defeat since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-edessa">Battle of Edessa</a>. It destroyed the core of the East Roman army and killed valuable administrators, and the lack of reserves worsened a recruitment crisis. It did not end the Empire, since imperial military power was only temporarily crippled.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, though partly tamed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, who accepted them once more as allied tribes, were never expelled, exterminated or assimilated. They remained a distinct entity within the frontiers, for a few years allies, later semi-independent or fully independent, and often hostile. The Therving Goths could now negotiate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a unified people inside the borders, and would become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>. This change in Rome's relationship with the barbarians would lead to the sack of Rome in 410.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> had been an Arian Christian, and his death helped pave the way for Theodosius to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a> the Empire's official form; the Goths, like many peoples beyond the frontier, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>. For the art of war, Charles Oman wrote in 1885 that the battle was a turning point, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> triumphing over Roman infantry and ushering in the age of the medieval knight. T. S. Burns disputed this in 1973, writing that the Gothic cavalry was fairly small, that Valens would have had more cavalry, and that the battle was mainly infantry against infantry.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 9 August 378 near Adrianople, Gothic rebels under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigern">Fritigern</a>, joined by cavalry that came back from foraging, routed the Eastern Roman army and the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> died. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> failed to take the city, and peace in 382 let them settle inside the Empire as allies.</em></p>

